<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Świdnica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Schweidnitz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Świdnica is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetes">Sudetes</a>. From the 1290s it was the capital of a Piast duchy, the last independent one in Silesia, and it grew rich from trade and brewing. Its Lutheran Church of Peace, the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swidnica.jpg" alt="Świdnica" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kateryna Baiduzha · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ratusz_w_%C5%9Awidnicy_z_drona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
